      "activities": [
        {
          "id": "monic-notes",
          "title": "From expansion to factorisation",
          "lede": "Learn to write a monic quadratic as a product of two linear factors. Work in your exercise book.",
          "notes": [
            {
              "term": "Before you begin",
              "body": "You should be able to multiply signed integers, list integer factor pairs, collect like terms and expand two brackets. Start by expanding (_x_ + 2)(_x_ + 3) in your book."
            },
            {
              "term": "Reverse the expansion",
              "body": "(_x_ + _m_)(_x_ + _n_) = _x_^2 + (_m_ + _n_)_x_ + _m_ × _n_. A monic quadratic has leading coefficient 1. To factorise _x_^2 + _b_ × _x_ + _c_, find integers with product c and sum b."
            },
            {
              "term": "Search systematically",
              "body": "First check for a common factor. Find two integers with the required product and sum, then write the brackets. If the pair is not clear, list distinct magnitude pairs and compare their signed sums."
            },
            {
              "term": "Choose the signs",
              "body": "If c is positive, the signs agree: both are positive when b is positive, and both are negative when b is negative. If c is negative, the signs differ; the greater absolute value takes the sign of b. If b is zero and c is negative, the two numbers must be opposites."
            },
            {
              "term": "What counts as a complete solution?",
              "body": "Record the selected pair and the factorisation, keeping every sign. Expansion is a useful check when you are unsure. These examples factorise over the integers; if no pair works, do not force a factorisation."
            }
          ]
        },
