      "title": "Factorising non-monic quadratics",
      "video": {
        "url": ""
      },
      "activities": [
        {
          "id": "nonmonic-notes",
          "title": "Why we split the middle term",
          "lede": "Extend the product-and-sum method to quadratics whose leading coefficient is not 1.",
          "notes": [
            {
              "term": "Start with the common factor",
              "body": "Write the expression in the form _a_ × _x_^2 + _b_ × _x_ + _c_, with a non-zero a. Remove the greatest common factor first. If the remaining leading coefficient is 1, use the monic method. In the non-monic examples here, the remaining leading coefficient is greater than 1."
            },
            {
              "term": "Product ac, sum b",
              "body": "Find integers m and n with _m_ × _n_ = _a_ × _c_ and _m_ + _n_ = _b_. Splitting _b_ × _x_ as _m_ × _x_ + _n_ × _x_ preserves the expression. The product condition makes the four terms suitable for factorising by grouping."
            },
            {
              "term": "Where the product comes from",
              "body": "Expanding (_p_ × _x_ + _q_)(_r_ × _x_ + _s_) gives _p_ × _r_ × _x_^2 + (_p_ × _s_ + _q_ × _r_)_x_ + _q_ × _s_. The split coefficients ps and qr add to b, and their product is (pr)(qs) = ac."
            },
            {
              "term": "Group, then factor",
              "body": "Group the first two and last two terms. Factor each group so that the bracketed expressions match, then factor out that shared bracket. A negative common factor may be needed. If the brackets do not match, recheck the pair and each sign."
            },
            {
              "term": "Do not skip the grouping",
              "body": "The selected pair splits the middle term; it is not usually the pair of constants in the final brackets. Keep the split and grouping in your written solution. Expand if you need to check."
            }
          ]
        },
